# Software Design

Companion to `ARCH.md`. Where `ARCH.md` describes hardware, peripherals, and pin
assignments, this doc describes how the firmware is organized: what runs in
which context, who owns what state, and the public API between layers.

---

## Execution Contexts

The firmware has **two parallel execution contexts** plus async event ISRs.
"Parallel" here means concurrent in the preemption sense — TIM1 ISR preempts
SysTick because it's higher priority. They share state through carefully
defined boundaries.

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ SM_PositionAndComm — SysTick @ 1 kHz, NVIC priority 15      │
│                                                              │
│   drive_sm_run()       — state transitions                  │
│   motion_pop()         — ring pop, sets pos_cmd/vel_cmd     │
│   ready_pin_update()   — READY signal to Pi                 │
│   position_step()      — position loop math → vel_cmd       │
│   telem_publish()      — snapshot to telemetry buffer       │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                            │ writes setpoints (iq_cmd, vel_cmd)
                            │ reads feedback (encoder, currents)
                            ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ SM_VelocityCurrent — TIM1_UP_TIM10 @ 20 kHz, NVIC priority 1│
│                                                              │
│   if (!drive_is_servo_on() && !drive_is_open_loop()) return;│
│   velocity_step()      — every 4th tick (5 kHz)             │
│   current_step()       — every tick (20 kHz)                │
│   pwm_apply()          — output to TIM1 CCR registers       │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Async event ISRs (set flags only, no state changes):
  DMA1_Stream3 (SPI2 RX)  — decode opcode, set request flag
  EXTI9_5      (nFAULT)   — drive_request_fault()
  EXTI15_10    (CS rising) — DMA rearm
```

