## SM_Drive — The Only Real State Machine

The drive is the only thing that has actual states (activities it can be
"doing"). Everything else is sequential code that runs every tick.

### States (activities)

| State              | Activity                                        |
|--------------------|-------------------------------------------------|
| `STATE_IDLE`       | Sitting, PWM disabled                           |
| `STATE_ALIGN`      | Applying d-axis voltage, locking rotor          |
| `STATE_OPEN_LOOP`  | Rotating voltage vector, no feedback            |
| `STATE_SERVO_ON`   | Closed-loop FOC, consuming trajectory from ring |
| `STATE_FAULT`      | Latched, PWM disabled, requires reset           |

### Transition diagram

```
                  servo_on_req
                       │
                ┌──────▼──────┐
                │    IDLE     │◄─────────────────────────┐
                └──┬──┬───────┘                          │
       open_loop_req│  │servo_on_req                     │
                ┌──▼┐ │ (future: align_req)              │
                │OL │ │                                  │
                └─┬─┘ │                                  │
       stop_req  │   │                                  │
                ┌▼───▼─────────┐ ring drained            │
                │  SERVO_ON    │─────────────────────────┘
                └──────┬───────┘
                       │ fault_req from any state
                       ▼
                ┌──────────────┐
                │    FAULT     │
                └──────────────┘
```

### Public API (drive.h)

**Status queries** — anyone can ask, returns current condition:

```c
bool drive_is_servo_on(void);
bool drive_is_open_loop(void);
bool drive_is_aligned(void);
bool drive_is_faulted(void);
bool drive_is_idle(void);

// For telemetry display only — not for branching logic
DriveState drive_get_state(void);
```

**Command requests** — called from ISRs, processed at next `drive_sm_run()`:

```c
void drive_request_servo_on(void);
void drive_request_open_loop(float v_mag, float d_theta);
void drive_request_stop(void);
void drive_request_fault(void);
```

**Tick entry point** — called once per SysTick:

```c
void drive_sm_run(void);   // replaces drive_update()
```

### Why status flags instead of state enum comparisons

Today: `if (drive_get_state() == STATE_SERVO_ON) { ... }` — scattered everywhere.

Tomorrow: `if (drive_is_servo_on()) { ... }` — cleaner, and the state enum
becomes implementation detail. If SERVO_ON later splits into
SERVO_ON_STARTING and SERVO_ON_RUNNING, `drive_is_servo_on()` returns true
for both. Callers never know.

